    "body": "> > Add ImageDataPixelFormat as an attribute to ImageData.\r\n> \r\n> This PR also adds the `pixelFormat` readonly attribute to `ImageData`, but I'm not completely sure why. [...] My concern is that it isn't necessary, but that it does add complexity just to validate/synchronize the type of the input source with the `pixelFormat` descriptor in the settings object.\r\n\r\nIndeed this is partially for future-proofing. This is intended to handle the situation where there isn't a 1:1 relationship between the `TypedArray` type and the actual pixel format (for now, there is indeed a 1:1 relationship). A simple example (but not the most useful) is that one could have an `rg-unorm8` format, which also uses `Uint8ClampedArray`, but only has two channels (red-green). There could also be things like grey-alpha as well, etc.\r\n\r\nIt's also there because it felt odd for the `ImageDataSettings` to have a `pixelFormat` for use by constructors that would then disappear and not be query-able.\r\n\r\nAnd it's also a good way of checking the actual layout (as opposed to the sub-type of the `ArrayBuffer`). Cause it will always be the case that `pixelFormat` implies a specific `ArrayBuffer` type.\r\n\r\n(And feature detection was sort of an afterthought).\r\n\r\n> I could imagine this being important if you wanted to accept a `Float16Array` yet force it to be treated like a `Uint8ClampedArray` by passing in `pixelFormat = \"rgba-unorm8\"`, but we don't seem to support that.\r\n\r\nIndeed, we would not want to support anything like that (float to uint8, etc).",
